WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:10.730
- Our final presentation
will be by Claire Barnes.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:14.180
Claire is a second year
masters of religion candidate

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.890
at Yale Divinity School,

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.513
concentrating in religion and ecology.

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:21.790
Claire has several fields of interest,

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:24.390
including food and
religion, climate justice,

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.730
indigenous representation in
International Climate Forums

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:29.810
and Gastronomical Archives.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.650
She currently works at
the Beinecke Library

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:35.640
and worked at the Yale
Divinity Special Collections

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:36.890
this past summer,

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.500
about which I think we're
going to be hearing some more.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.240
(chuckles) Claire also holds a masters

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.770
in international political theory

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:44.200
from the University of St. Andrews

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.270
and a bachelor's in
religion and philosophy

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.997
from Emory University, please
join me in welcoming Claire.

00:00:49.997 --> 00:00:52.997
(audience clapping)

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:56.023
- Hi everyone.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:00.090
So as Matthew said, my
name is Claire Barnes

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:04.450
and I study food and
religion at Yale Divinity,

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:07.880
particularly religion and ecology.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.340
And I'm here to give, let's
see if I can get this to work,

00:01:13.049 --> 00:01:14.500
duh duh duh

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.670
a general highlight from the collection.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.830
I think as a student
worker in the collection,

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.360
I would love it if more people (giggles)

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:22.980
would visit the collection,

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:27.540
especially working within
smaller archival spaces,

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.060
it's quite rewarding for
both students and faculty.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:35.660
And then I'm gonna be
focusing on some research

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.670
that I conducted with Chloe Star,

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:42.270
who teaches this class
called China Missions.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.630
And so talking about the ways
in which students utilize

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.900
the collections in a classroom setting,

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:51.550
but also kind of untangling
some of the questions

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.670
and possibilities within
missionary archives.

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:56.540
And then further areas of study,

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:01.070
which are currently still being cataloged

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:04.640
in the Andover Newton
Collection, which has been added

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.953
to the Yale Divinity School's Collection.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.430
So the history of the Yale
Divinity School Collections

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:14.980
is that there are 6,000 linear
feet of archival material

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.113
in our collections,

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.888
which ranges from pamphlets
to microform holdings.

00:02:21.888 --> 00:02:26.740
We also have several areas of
specialization, for example,

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.570
which I will talk about later,
the China missions project,

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:32.530
which many of the China missionary papers,

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:36.520
and as well as Bibles and
translations of Bibles

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.930
are held in this project,
and they were acquired

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:41.763
in connection with the
China Records Project.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.730
The Himalayan Mission Archive Collection,

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.450
and was formed in 2008

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:52.160
as an amalgamation of four
different archival organizations.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.580
And it was transferred to
the Yale Divinity Collection

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.900
from the Center For The
Study of World Christianity

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.450
at the University of Edinburgh.

00:03:00.765 --> 00:03:04.450
And then finally the Divinity
School also has a rich holding

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.030
of archives that have been transferred

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:09.430
from the Andover Newton
Theological School.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.120
And these are still being cataloged

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.150
and there's room in this collection

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:18.553
for additional scholarship.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:20.910
For example,

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:25.880
here is a papyri from the
Andover Newton collection.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.780
The papyri collection
from the Andover Newton,

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:32.000
collection ranges from
1st to 6th century CE,

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.140
but this is from the early 4th century CE.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:37.870
The collection also
contains personal papers,

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.660
archives of organizations,
pamphlets and papers,

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:43.633
and missionary postcards,
images, and ephemera.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:49.410
We also have a really
interesting collection

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:54.410
of translated Bibles and also Bible folio.

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:56.070
So these are big Bibles

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:58.850
that would be used in a pulpit setting.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:01.950
So the Bible on that, and
you're seeing right now

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.100
on the right is quite huge, quite large.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:06.180
It's like this big, and these are on-site

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:10.950
and thinking about the ways in which,

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.660
how can we critically
analyze missionary culture?

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:17.420
We can think about the
intersection between linguistics

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.730
and visual culture within
these pieces for example,

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.780
a lot of these Bibles are
also illuminated manuscripts.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.590
So you can see here,

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:28.623
there's an image of Genesis
that has been illustrated,

00:04:29.777 --> 00:04:33.290
and thinking about the continuity
between our collections

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:34.990
in terms of visual culture,

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:39.330
there are also contemporary
forms of visual culture

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.430
that are quite interesting
in the collection.

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.610
These are two of my favorite pieces

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:45.830
from the collection
besides the work that I did

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:47.223
in Chloe Star's class.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.010
So on the right, you can see here,

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:54.780
they're Chick Tracts and
Chick Tracts are comic,

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:59.780
little comic books that were
illustrated by Jack T. Chick

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:02.680
since the early 1960s.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:07.230
And they document a fringe
evangelical responses

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:09.580
to upheavals in American culture.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:12.220
And they're often placed in library

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:14.520
so students who will go
into libraries will see

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:16.240
these Chick Tract collections,

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.930
and they'll be inclined by their titles

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:23.747
such as global warming or
doom town to look at them,

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:29.150
but they actually are quite fundamental

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:32.200
are fundamentalist and
they're quite conservative.

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:33.880
But then on the other end of the spectrum,

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:37.170
we have these really interesting
pins from the collection

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:40.460
that also kind of, we can
think about the evolution

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:44.300
of religious visual culture in America,

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:46.839
particularly with these pins.

00:05:46.839 --> 00:05:51.839
And these are from the Henry
Hale Butler Junior papers,

00:05:52.091 --> 00:05:56.640
and these pins range from 1936 to 2013.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:00.227
And as you can see, this
is one of my favorite pins,

00:06:00.227 --> 00:06:02.910
"School of America, U.S.
Government Terrorist Training."

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:06.417
And it's not a cue for
people to think that,

00:06:06.417 --> 00:06:08.197
"Oh, we're gonna have an
interesting conversation

00:06:08.197 --> 00:06:09.840
"about global warming and
the evangelical papers."

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.440
And actually it'll be
something quite different.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:16.380
But these are pins that
were collected by Butler

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.940
kind of in response to many things.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:21.720
So for example, you can
see Jail Bush right there,

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:24.440
and there's conversations within

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:25.840
that we can have around these pins

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:28.940
in terms of both spiritual understandings

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:31.908
of American cultural shifts
and social movements,

00:06:31.908 --> 00:06:35.460
but also in terms of kind
of a continuity thinking

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:39.793
about missionary culture
in America as well.

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:43.850
And then I think as a
student in the archives,

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:45.810
at least from a student perspective,

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:47.660
our archives are a great place

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:51.140
to think about
methodological uncertainties,

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:53.940
questions and possibilities
for missionary papers,

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:58.940
thinking about holding both
a criticism of the work

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:04.330
in our collection as
inter-tangled and colonial

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:05.690
and Imperial matrices.

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:09.210
And then also thinking about
what these pieces are saying

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:12.930
and what the missionaries are reporting on

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:14.010
in the places that they are.

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:17.630
So for example, if we can
think about geopolitical shifts

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:19.990
in the countries that
these missionaries are in

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:21.770
and what they're, how
they think about them,

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:23.680
how they think about their
friends and their families

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:24.570
in these countries.

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:26.577
And so holding both of those realities

00:07:26.577 --> 00:07:29.760
and thinking Lamin Sanneh was a professor

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:33.850
of World Christianity at Yale Divinity.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:35.770
And he in particular was interested

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:38.010
in looking at some of the linguistic work

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:42.013
that missionaries had done
and thinking about broadening

00:07:42.013 --> 00:07:46.570
our use and understanding
of missionary papers,

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:48.450
thinking about them both critically

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:50.880
and thinking about their possibilities.

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:53.600
And the Yale in a book
group on World Christianity

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:56.140
in the History of Missions
continues his work

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:59.720
in this line of thinking
critically about our pieces,

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:01.660
not only in terms of their provenance,

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:03.940
but in terms of American imperialism

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:07.230
and also critical things
that we can gain from them

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:10.890
and in terms of social
and religious commentary,

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:15.250
and they meet every year and
they're also meeting in 2022.

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:18.150
So that will be if y'all are interested

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:23.150
in learning more about the
development of missionary studies

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:27.840
as a discipline, and also
rebranded as World Christianity,

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:31.820
thinking about disciplinary
boundaries within the archives

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:33.303
is quite interesting.

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:39.220
So now I'm gonna turn to my
specific research project

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:42.730
in China Missions, which
was with Chloe Star again,

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:46.410
who is an expert in
Chinese Christian theology

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:51.260
and has published an excellent
book on Chinese theologians.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:54.210
But when I had entered her class,

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:56.240
which was an interesting hybrid class

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.240
between an exhibition style
of looking at both Jesuit,

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:03.110
like early Jesuit missionaries,

00:09:03.110 --> 00:09:07.720
who were in China and as well
as translations of the Bible

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:08.983
in Chinese characters.

00:09:10.196 --> 00:09:11.980
So that was kinda the
first part of the class.

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:14.680
And then the second part
of the class was dedicated

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:17.050
to individual research in the archives.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:20.390
And so when I was thinking
about what I normally study,

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:23.620
which is religion and food
in an American context,

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:26.570
she had pointed me and Joan
and everyone in the archives

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:30.260
had helped me get to the Albert
and Celia Steward's Papers

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:33.020
and particular addendum
that had been brought

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:36.650
to Yale Divinity and had
not yet been processed.

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:40.270
So Albert Steward was
trained as a biologist,

00:09:40.270 --> 00:09:43.560
but then served as a university professor

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:45.910
at the Christian College at
the University of Nanjing.

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:48.220
At that point it was spelled with a K

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:50.100
and you can trace the
evolution of the college

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:52.740
actually in its synthesis
with other universities,

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:55.770
from Republican era and
to the communist era.

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:57.980
But he taught at this university,

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:02.980
he taught botany and he
ran the herbarium there.

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:06.800
And he was there from 1921 to 1950

00:10:08.022 --> 00:10:11.679
and was also like intern
during the Japanese invasion.

00:10:11.679 --> 00:10:16.500
But I had focused my research
project on his papers,

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:20.400
and I was interested in
looking at the development

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:23.630
of what is called an
agricultural missionary

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:27.810
and thinking about these intersections

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:31.320
between agriculture,
religion, and national memory

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:36.150
and development in his collection as well,

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:38.040
there are a wide variety of things.

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:42.020
So he has like diaries, in the addendum,

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:43.690
which I processed actually,

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:48.160
he has ration cards from
when he was interned

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:50.560
and he has letters and whatnot.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:53.610
And there are also lantern
slides and lantern slides

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:56.810
are small glass slides
that you put in a projector

00:10:56.810 --> 00:10:58.190
and they're actually quite heavy.

00:10:58.190 --> 00:10:59.170
So it's interesting

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:01.400
to think that they
would carry these around

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.960
to both document to their families.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:06.010
Oh, like, this is where
I teach, for example,

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:08.060
this is a image of the campus,

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:10.180
or these are some of the challenges

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:11.700
that I'm facing in my work.

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:15.570
So in this right slide, we can see here,

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:19.260
it's labeled "Wasteful
cutting of forests."

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:22.600
But actually if we dive
deeper into his collection,

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:27.135
we can see that he is engaging

00:11:27.135 --> 00:11:31.140
with different political
and policing bodies

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:33.710
during the KMT government

00:11:33.710 --> 00:11:38.230
when this photograph is
actually documenting a moment

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:41.230
where this is a research forest,

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:45.610
and there were locals who
came and chop down the wood

00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:48.500
for fuel because there
was resource shortages

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:50.740
during Japanese invasion.

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:54.510
And so he had to engage with
different policing bodies

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:56.720
within Nanjing at that time

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:01.240
to kind of balance his agricultural work

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:04.090
with the local conditions.

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:07.780
And it was quite
interesting about his work

00:12:07.780 --> 00:12:12.780
is that he thinks about
what is often termed

00:12:13.660 --> 00:12:18.130
by the university as the
Christianization of National Life

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:19.760
with these Christian colleges,

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:22.690
he thinks about his work in terms of land.

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:27.160
And so he has this interesting fusion

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:31.980
of thinking about his
religious experience,

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:35.840
and his experience teaching with his work

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:37.210
outside of the university,

00:12:37.210 --> 00:12:40.680
because oftentimes both him
and his Chinese colleagues

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:44.790
would go out into the more local areas

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:47.320
and they would either gather plants

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:52.200
or they would work on local
conditions if there was droughts

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:53.323
or whatnot.

00:12:54.888 --> 00:12:59.750
And we have a collection
thinking about my research

00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:01.530
in terms of just his papers,

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:05.210
I think wasn't proving fruitful enough.

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:09.200
So then looking at other
pieces of the collection,

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:14.040
we have a repository of
agriculture and forestry notes,

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:15.950
which is at the college
that he was teaching at

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:20.950
within the university and
thinking the university itself

00:13:22.070 --> 00:13:25.990
was positioning its work

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:29.970
in terms of a larger
agricultural policy for China.

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:34.030
And it would document
all of the professors

00:13:34.030 --> 00:13:39.030
that had either been engaging
with agricultural policy talks

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:44.100
with the shifting geopolitical
lines and entities.

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:47.690
And then they were also very concerned

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:50.120
and he existed within this university

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:52.540
that was very concerned
about government recognition

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:53.980
of the university.

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:58.510
And so thinking about his
work in particular for me,

00:13:58.510 --> 00:14:03.130
was quite useful that he
had joined a department

00:14:03.130 --> 00:14:06.133
with nation building aspirations as well.

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:10.040
Okay.

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:14.527
And I think that he collected newspapers

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:20.880
from both American newspapers
and Chinese newspapers,

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:26.210
that document important
moments and diplomatic moments,

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:31.210
for example, this is at the
advent of during World War II.

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:36.520
And so thinking about
his role as a missionary

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:39.560
in this complex web of
his agricultural work,

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:44.560
and as well as the histories
and the developing histories

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:50.200
of not only China, but in
where he was a missionary,

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:55.060
but of the United States
and of political relations.

00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:59.900
And I think what was really
important for our class

00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:04.900
is teasing out our
understanding of missionary work

00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:06.740
in different places.

00:15:06.740 --> 00:15:09.011
And so in this example,

00:15:09.011 --> 00:15:13.826
I was able to look at kind of
this complex web and matrices

00:15:13.826 --> 00:15:18.826
of his life to think about
how we can push the boundaries

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:22.203
of missionary work in world
Christianity in general.

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:26.810
And also what has been
really quite rewarding for me

00:15:26.810 --> 00:15:28.570
in terms of this work,

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:31.340
not only thinking about
like agricultural histories,

00:15:31.340 --> 00:15:36.340
which I don't think we normally
connect with missionaries,

00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:40.100
but which I think is quite
relevant in terms of colonization

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:44.070
and imperialization, both here and abroad,

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:48.500
but that the donor for his collection

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:49.880
was his son David Steward.

00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:53.700
And he was able to engage
with my presentation

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:58.700
and add on points when I had
done my final presentation

00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:00.780
for our class.

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:04.530
And so I think that in
a smaller archival space

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:07.530
that is in a larger library network

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:11.360
to think about the relational
aspects of our work

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:12.573
is quite important.

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:16.920
And we still email today and he adds

00:16:18.310 --> 00:16:20.440
I think great insights
into some of the work

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:21.750
that I had been doing,

00:16:21.750 --> 00:16:24.510
particularly around his
father's orientation,

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:26.683
towards his teaching and his work.

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:32.860
And also on his experience
as a kid growing up in China

00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:39.050
at that point, and thinking
about also the family legacy,

00:16:40.271 --> 00:16:42.643
and what as a student worker,

00:16:45.032 --> 00:16:47.250
which I had transitioned
to after this project,

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:48.870
it made me think about,

00:16:48.870 --> 00:16:52.420
in what ways can we
better archive our work?

00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:54.670
And in what ways can we handle our work

00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:58.640
so that it reflects not
only the complexities

00:17:00.740 --> 00:17:05.740
of the donor's life but also
of his continued family legacy.

00:17:10.610 --> 00:17:14.770
And his son had actually been a professor

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:16.700
and he was quite interested

00:17:16.700 --> 00:17:20.230
in seeing the development of this work.

00:17:20.230 --> 00:17:23.400
And so I think that is a
great opportunity for students

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:28.400
in particular to be involved
with the donors understanding

00:17:29.150 --> 00:17:32.563
of their own work and their own donation.

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:37.620
Agricultural histories and
Chinese missionary work

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:40.140
are also very tied to medical histories.

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:44.030
And this is an example of
another paper that I worked on

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:46.860
in which these are the
Edward L. Bliss Papers

00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:51.665
that I cataloged in addition
to the Steward Papers

00:17:51.665 --> 00:17:54.283
and helped with the finding aid for,

00:17:55.660 --> 00:17:57.440
and this is a really interesting case

00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:02.440
because I had a lecture in
medicine reached out to me

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:07.660
and had asked, "Are there any
connections in these Papers

00:18:08.237 --> 00:18:12.110
"between agriculture, and
medicine, and religion?"

00:18:12.110 --> 00:18:13.930
And so this is another example

00:18:13.930 --> 00:18:15.930
of kind of the interdisciplinary work

00:18:15.930 --> 00:18:20.002
that we can do when we
tease out the material

00:18:20.002 --> 00:18:21.280
in these Papers.

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:25.150
And this is Bliss with his
students and colleagues,

00:18:25.150 --> 00:18:28.520
and then these are examples
of what is in his collection.

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:33.520
So from photographs, to letters,
to passports which we have.

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:38.560
So the Yale Divinity Special Collections

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:41.960
are not only a repository
for this rich collection

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:46.960
of Chinese material and missionary papers,

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:51.470
but they're also a repository
for the history of YDS.

00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:55.080
And so the Women's Center
Material is the third collection

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:57.923
that I was able to work
on as a student worker.

00:19:00.207 --> 00:19:02.950
And this collection was
actually quite large.

00:19:02.950 --> 00:19:06.420
It was 30 boxes compared
to Stewards collection

00:19:06.420 --> 00:19:11.420
was about seven boxes,
but Joan Bates Forsberg,

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:14.110
who was the founder of the Women's Center

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:20.566
was quite heavily involved
in creating a network

00:19:20.566 --> 00:19:25.566
of female seminarians to
understand their role,

00:19:25.840 --> 00:19:28.830
not only as students, but in response to,

00:19:28.830 --> 00:19:32.010
as I've mentioned with the
Buttons and the Chick Tracts

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:36.650
in response to different
moments in American history.

00:19:36.650 --> 00:19:39.030
So different waves of
feminism, for example,

00:19:39.030 --> 00:19:42.660
and also during, there're lots of material

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:46.780
in this collection on women's responses

00:19:46.780 --> 00:19:51.397
to the Vietnam War and further industrial,

00:19:54.296 --> 00:19:57.780
like the Military Industrial
Complex that it's development.

00:19:57.780 --> 00:20:00.270
And they were very focused

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:03.700
on not only curricular advancements

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:08.440
for using more inclusive
language in their curriculum,

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:12.690
but just creating an
all-inclusive voice for women,

00:20:12.690 --> 00:20:14.590
which right now is defined by the Centers,

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:16.763
anyone who self identifies as a woman.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:21.420
And the current YDS Women's
Center is still in existence.

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:24.670
And they publish a journal called "Voice,"

00:20:24.670 --> 00:20:27.763
which is fitting for their mission,

00:20:29.556 --> 00:20:32.273
and we continue to
collect copies of Voice.

00:20:33.939 --> 00:20:37.460
And so this is another way
in which the YDS archive

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:43.230
can be a great resource for
students who are interested

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:47.563
in the development of
YDS as an institution.

00:20:49.050 --> 00:20:51.160
And then these are
further areas for study.

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:55.970
These are both medieval
manuscripts from 1500s,

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:59.000
and these are in the
Andover Newton Collection.

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:04.000
This is illustrated biblical
texts from Ezekiel, I believe.

00:21:05.700 --> 00:21:09.750
And then the image on the
right is a cabalistic text

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:14.110
that has a combination we
were looking at it yesterday

00:21:14.110 --> 00:21:17.133
of Hebrew and other languages.

00:21:18.281 --> 00:21:23.281
And so thinking about the role
of smaller archival spaces

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:27.227
and really diving and
giving access to students,

00:21:27.227 --> 00:21:30.410
to these novel pieces

00:21:30.410 --> 00:21:33.100
that most people haven't delved into yet.

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:36.780
And so people can...
students in particular,

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:39.830
working with professors that
have a broader understanding,

00:21:39.830 --> 00:21:44.350
both linguistically and
materially of the collection

00:21:44.350 --> 00:21:46.490
can have a quite personalized experience

00:21:46.490 --> 00:21:51.490
in the YDS archives thinking
about there are many avenues

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:53.140
and rooms for discovery in the archive,

00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:56.600
as I hope you've seen
through this collection

00:21:56.600 --> 00:21:58.170
as well as my own research

00:21:59.344 --> 00:22:01.600
and there's room for relational building

00:22:01.600 --> 00:22:03.230
within the archive in this way.

00:22:03.230 --> 00:22:07.443
And so here is my contact information.

00:22:08.530 --> 00:22:11.160
And if you'd like to contact
me, that is my email,

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:13.970
Joan Duffy is a senior
archivist in Yale archives

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:16.650
who can help you piece through

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:19.940
any of your interests that
you have in the collection.

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:24.510
And then Suzanne Estelle-Holmer
is our main librarian

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:27.490
and she has developed
excellent research guides

00:22:27.490 --> 00:22:30.328
for further interdisciplinary connections

00:22:30.328 --> 00:22:34.450
between contemporary texts
in our archival material.

00:22:34.450 --> 00:22:35.763
So thank you so much.

00:22:36.631 --> 00:22:39.631
(audience clapping)

00:22:43.543 --> 00:22:45.960
(indistinct)

00:22:47.334 --> 00:22:48.167
- [Audience] Happy to start with one.

00:22:48.167 --> 00:22:50.100
You mentioned cataloging

00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:51.280
when you were talking
about the Bliss Papers.

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:52.113
- Mm-hmm.

00:22:52.113 --> 00:22:53.050
- [Audience] And I think
it's really interesting

00:22:53.050 --> 00:22:54.630
what you were saying
about donor engagement.

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:56.890
Have you thought about,
I mean, we're in a moment

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:58.900
right where we're thinking
about how we catalog

00:22:58.900 --> 00:23:01.180
and how objects have been
cataloged historically.

00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:03.790
Can you talk a little bit
about your work in cataloging?

00:23:03.790 --> 00:23:06.790
And you're working with a lot of materials

00:23:06.790 --> 00:23:11.100
that come into play with
current social movements

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:14.460
and other things and how you
sort of navigate that task.

00:23:14.460 --> 00:23:18.920
- Yeah, so when I was
first... so my class project

00:23:18.920 --> 00:23:21.990
on the Steward Papers was
almost a hybrid project

00:23:21.990 --> 00:23:26.900
where I was able to delve
into agricultural histories

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:28.950
through his lens,

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:33.330
but also I learned how to
internally catalog materials

00:23:33.330 --> 00:23:36.720
based off of Yale Divinity
standards and external standards.

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:40.000
But I think that, particularly for me

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:43.190
thinking about provenance is
a really interesting question

00:23:43.190 --> 00:23:46.650
and thinking about for the
evolution of world Christianity

00:23:46.650 --> 00:23:49.523
or missionary studies how
that might come into play.

00:23:51.426 --> 00:23:54.850
And I think that as a young researcher,

00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:58.710
I think having fresh eyes
on research collections

00:23:58.710 --> 00:24:01.660
is really important because we
bring in these considerations

00:24:01.660 --> 00:24:04.790
and these concerns to the archive space,

00:24:04.790 --> 00:24:07.340
and then it becomes an
intergenerational task

00:24:07.340 --> 00:24:11.360
of figuring out how do
we uphold the voices

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:14.460
and the narratives that are
in the papers themselves,

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:15.490
such as Albert Steward,

00:24:15.490 --> 00:24:18.820
but also how do we interpret
them to make them meaningful

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:22.920
for our current context and
for our current critiques

00:24:22.920 --> 00:24:25.503
and also the possibilities
of missionary work.

00:24:26.430 --> 00:24:28.830
So I think hopefully that
answers your question.

00:24:33.313 --> 00:24:34.813
